CAT = "nscaIntake"; SEG = "INT"
Q = [
("At the first meeting, what is the trainer mainly checking for besides the client's goals?",
 'Trainer-client compatibility', ['Client payment history', 'Preferred gym membership tier', 'Favorite workout music'],
 'The initial interview establishes rapport, identifies goals and decides whether this trainer suits this client. A poor fit is better found early and referred on.'),
('A client wants Olympic lifting coaching the trainer has never studied. Best step?',
 'Refer to a qualified coach', ['Learn it during the sessions', 'Teach the lifts from videos', 'Swap in machine exercises'],
 "Compatibility includes the trainer's competence for the goal. Referring to a qualified specialist protects the client and keeps the trainer within their expertise."),
('Which tool asks whether a client is ready to start an exercise program?',
 'Readiness questionnaire', ['Food frequency record', 'Body mass index chart', 'Liability waiver form'],
 'An attitude and readiness inventory shows how prepared the client is to change. A waiver addresses legal risk, not readiness.'),
('Which form tells the client what the program involves and its possible risks?',
 'Informed consent', ['Lifestyle questionnaire', 'Physician release', 'Session log'],
 'Informed consent explains procedures, risks and benefits and records that the client agreed. A physician release comes from the doctor, not the client.'),
('A liability waiver mainly does what?',
 'Limits claims for ordinary negligence', ['Proves the client is medically healthy', "Replaces the physician's medical release", 'Lists every exercise in the program'],
 'By signing a waiver, a client gives up some right to claim for ordinary negligence where the law allows it. It never covers gross negligence and says nothing about health.'),
('Which detail belongs in the client-trainer agreement?',
 'Cancellation policy', ['Family medical history', 'Resting heart rate reading', 'Body fat percentage'],
 'The agreement sets expectations: session times, fees, cancellations and conduct. Health details belong on the health appraisal form.'),
('A client asks to train in a small group to save money. What should the trainer weigh first?',
 'Whether the format suits their goals', ['Which small group has the best music', 'How quickly the small groups fill up', 'Whether the client owns home weights'],
 "The 2025 DCO asks the trainer to match the format (one-on-one, small group or performance group) and duration to the client's needs. Cost matters, but suitability comes first."),
("A client's brother had a heart attack at age 48. Where does this belong?",
 'Family medical history', ['Lifestyle questionnaire', 'Personal injury history', 'Daily dietary log'],
 'Early cardiac events in first-degree relatives are family history, part of the health appraisal. Brothers count when the event comes before 55.'),
('A health form shows the client smoked until four months ago. How is this treated?',
 'As a current risk factor', ['As no cardiac risk at all', 'As a family history item', 'As a past injury only'],
 'Quitting within the previous six months still counts as smoking for cardiovascular risk screening. After six months it is treated as former smoking.'),
("Which client answer most clearly needs a physician's release before training?",
 'Chest pain on exertion', ['Mild knee stiffness at dawn', 'Wants to lose 10 pounds', 'Has not lifted in a year'],
 'Chest discomfort with effort is a sign of possible heart disease and calls for medical clearance. The other answers shape the program but need no release.'),
('A client lists a beta-blocker on the health form. How does it affect training?',
 'Heart rate rises less with effort', ['Heart rate rises faster with effort', 'Blood sugar drops sharply in sessions', 'Joints become much more flexible'],
 'Beta-blockers blunt the heart rate response, so heart rate targets mislead. Rating of perceived exertion is the better intensity guide.'),
('What should the trainer do with a completed medical history form?',
 'Interpret it before testing', ['File it after the first month', 'Ask the client to keep a copy at home', 'Send it on to the gym owner'],
 'The form is read and interpreted before any fitness evaluation so that risks shape what is tested. It is then kept securely.'),
('A client reports a torn rotator cuff last year, now discharged. What helps most?',
 'Ask what the therapist advised', ['Avoid all upper-body work for good', 'Test a 1RM press in the first session', 'Treat it as healed and ignore it'],
 'Seeking input from the treating professional tells the trainer what is safe now. Avoiding all upper-body work is more than the history requires.'),
('Why should a trainer build a network of allied health professionals?',
 'To refer clients when needed', ['To share client files freely', 'To sell supplements together', 'To diagnose injuries faster'],
 'The DCO asks trainers to establish and work with a network (nutrition, mental health, rehabilitation, medicine) so clients reach the right expert. Sharing files still needs consent.'),
('A client wants weight-loss help and reports eating very little and fainting. Who should they see?',
 'Physician or registered dietitian', ['A supplement company salesperson', 'A licensed massage therapist', 'Another certified personal trainer'],
 "Fainting with very low intake is a medical and nutrition concern beyond a trainer's scope. A physician or registered dietitian is the right referral."),
('Which item is part of a lifestyle questionnaire rather than a medical history?',
 'Typical hours of sleep', ['Past surgeries', 'Current diagnoses', 'Prescribed medications and doses'],
 'Lifestyle questions cover habits such as sleep, stress, alcohol, smoking and activity. Surgeries, diagnoses and medications are medical history.'),
("A client's physician returns a release that limits heart rate to 120 bpm. What follows?",
 'Program within that limit', ['Ignore it after week two', 'Raise it if the client agrees', 'Ask another trainer to decide'],
 'A medical release with restrictions sets the boundaries of the program. Only the physician can change them.'),
('Which question best opens an initial interview with active listening?',
 'What brings you in today?', ['Can you train five days?', 'Is your goal weight loss?', 'Do you prefer free weights?'],
 'An open question invites the client to explain in their own words. Closed or leading questions narrow the answer before the trainer understands the goal.'),
("Restating a client's concern in your own words mainly shows:",
 'That you heard them', ['That you disagree with them', 'That you are the expert here', 'That time is short'],
 'Paraphrasing is an active listening skill that confirms understanding and builds rapport.'),
('A new client says they have trained for eight years. This mostly informs the trainer about:',
 'Training age', ['Biological age', 'Chronological age', 'Resting heart rate'],
 'Training age is the number of years of structured training. The 2025 DCO lists it with biological age as part of the structural evaluation.'),
]
